more public appearances, you know, not doing

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.720
as good as he used to.
Speaker 1: Yeah, but it's not. It's not because,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.439
I mean, the when you have an incumbent running
for reelection.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:10.530
Speaker 3: You don't do the.
Speaker 2: Big if he does go to debate and

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.420
he gets he gets, you know trials that just
makes him look even worse as the incumbent.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.569
So people would be more likely to vote otherwise.
I completely understand what you're saying.

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:27.700
Yeah. All right. My second thing was about
the Florida Senate Bill 7050, where they slipped

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:35.560
in the fact that you can now run for other
office if you're running for the presidency.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:41.970
Speaker 1: I actually did not see that. But
I know that there was a discussion of changing

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:48.590
that. And of course, it's specifically for
for Ron DeSantis. Yeah, I didn't I actually

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:52.450
didn't see that that passed.
Speaker 2: Yeah. Farron Cousins actually does

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:57.870
video about it. It was last Friday, I believe
that they put it through, of course, behind

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:03.840
closed doors, saying that if you were to run
for office in other other offices like that,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.780
would you'd have to resign first, resign to
run. And then they put in if you're going

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.760
for the presidency now, that's totally cool.
Go for it.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.230
Speaker 1: Yeah, well, I mean, listen, we
all know the whole point of that. I still

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.930
don't know 100% whether DeSantis is or isn't
going to announce. I mean, I think he probably

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.209
will, but I think it's possible he'll get
talked out of it. But it's very clear that

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:27.800
they're setting the stage for him to run if
he wants to.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:35.540
Speaker 2: Well, if he does, it might pull
away a little bit of some Republican votes,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.860
but we'll see what happens in 24. Thanks for
taking my call.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:44.230
Speaker 1: All right, David from Florida.
There he goes. Let's go next to L.J. from

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.910
Las Vegas. L.J. from Las Vegas. Welcome to
the program. What's on your mind?

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.540
Speaker 3: Hey, David. How you been.
Speaker 1: Doing? Well.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:58.060
Speaker 3: Good. And I have a kind of a rough
cut on some things. So it's not 100% thought

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.390
through. But I just kind of want to get your
thought on it, if that's cool. Sure. So I

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.910
know we always talk about like every single
year the black community being a very big

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:12.750
part of the Democratic Party and the like.
One of the most reliable voting blocs for,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.810
you know, Democrats, in a sense. Yes. I kind
of wondered what some of the things that we

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:22.950
can do because I'm Hispanic myself. Right.
To activate more Hispanic people, more Mexicans,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:32.280
more Cubans, more just the block in general,
to kind of create more, you know, discussion

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.780
around like, you know, opening the border
for people who are good. You know, And again,

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:41.380
this is, again, a a rough cut of our ideas.
I just kind of want to get your point of view

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.539
on that.
Speaker 1: Well, here's the thing. One of

00:04:43.539 --> 00:04:50.850
the things that I try to resist is assuming
that in order to get a certain group involved,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:05:00.000
you need to cater or pander with particular
issues above and beyond the general idea that

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.860
there are all sorts of different reasons why
they should be engaged with politics. Now,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:10.449
obviously we can't ignore that different groups
have different priorities. So if a population

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:15.960
that is more likely to have family in Central
and South America is almost certainly going

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:22.139
to be more motivated by certain issues than
people that don't, and that might relate to

00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:27.569
everything from so-called chain migration
to dark, permanent status, all these different

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:33.000
things. But one of the things I think is really
important is if we identify not necessarily

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.100
a group, but people who aren't engaging politically,
first, we should be having a general conversation

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:44.009
about why aren't you engaged, why aren't you
voting, whatever the case may be, before automatically

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:50.210
jumping to like, for example, if there were
a bunch of, say, gay men who weren't voting,

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:56.450
I wouldn't necessarily go straight in on LGBT
rights, for example. I would first just want

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.870
to know, why aren't you? Why aren't you engaged?
Because the reason they're not engaged may

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:04.729
actually not be related specifically to issues
of LGBT rights. And obviously it's important

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:09.200
to say, Hey, it might be of particular importance
for you to get involved, because right now

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.690
one party actually wants to strip rights specifically
from folks based on an identity that you share.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.100
But I would start with the more general reasons
to be involved politically.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:23.500
Speaker 3: Hmm. Definitely. It's just it's
interesting because as someone who grew up

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:30.610
here in Vegas, right in Nevada, it's 20% of
the population is Hispanic. Right. And within

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.319
Nevada State, according to the Census Bureau.
So, you know, I don't know how accurate that

00:06:35.319 --> 00:06:37.200
is, but that's at least our numbers that I
can recall.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.289
Speaker 1: Yeah, I don't have them in front
of me. I'll take your word for it.

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:46.090
Speaker 3: Yeah. But in that sense, right.
I do feel like there's always this assumption

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:52.169
that the Hispanic vote, or at least in my
personal view, that people don't think it's

00:06:52.169 --> 00:06:57.460
as important or as valued as, let's say, the
black vote or the mothers in suburban neighborhoods.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:06.130
Right. And my thought is, why couldn't there
be a discussion more to include Hispanic families?

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:10.629
Nevada, or at least Las Vegas, has one of
the best unions in the entire country. Right.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:15.810
The Culinary Workers Union. Yes. And I feel
like as Hispanics, that's a very big thing

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.470
that we should be focusing on is increasing
the size of the union and using Nevada as

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.000
a template for why unions should work across
the country.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.740
Speaker 1: I agree 100%. And I as you know,
I was born in Argentina, moved to the U.S.,

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:36.340
Hispanic, immigrant, etc.. I agree with you
100% that it is a group that I think should

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:43.250
be. It's not a question of targeting, but
understood more. And I completely agree with

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.950
everything you're saying.
Speaker 3: Cool. Thank you. Appreciate it.

